      {type:"Very Short",q:"17. What is calcination?",a:"Heating a carbonate ore in limited or no air."},
      {type:"Very Short",q:"18. What is anode mud?",a:"Insoluble impurities that collect below the anode during electrolytic refining."},
      {type:"Very Short",q:"19. What is an amphoteric oxide?",a:"An oxide that reacts with both acids and bases."},
      {type:"Very Short",q:"20. Name two amphoteric oxides.",a:"Aluminium oxide, Al₂O₃, and zinc oxide, ZnO."},

      {type:"Short",q:"21. State four general physical properties of metals.",a:"Metals are generally lustrous, malleable, ductile and good conductors of heat and electricity. Exceptions exist."},
      {type:"Short",q:"22. Why are sodium and potassium stored in kerosene?",a:"They react vigorously with oxygen and moisture and may catch fire. Kerosene keeps them away from air and water."},
      {type:"Short",q:"23. Why does ionic compound formation involve electron transfer?",a:"A metal commonly loses electrons to form positive ions, while a non-metal gains electrons to form negative ions. Electrostatic attraction holds the ions together."},
      {type:"Short",q:"24. Why do ionic compounds have high melting points?",a:"Strong electrostatic forces between oppositely charged ions require considerable energy to overcome."},
      {type:"Short",q:"25. Why do ionic compounds conduct electricity when molten but not usually when solid?",a:"In a molten state their ions can move and carry charge. In a solid crystal, the ions are fixed in position."},
      {type:"Short",q:"26. Differentiate between roasting and calcination.",a:"Roasting heats sulphide ores in excess air. Calcination heats carbonate ores in limited or no air."},
      {type:"Short",q:"27. What is galvanisation?",a:"It is coating iron or steel with zinc to reduce corrosion."},
      {type:"Short",q:"28. Why is copper used in electrical wiring?",a:"Copper is a good electrical conductor and is ductile, so it can be drawn into wires."},
      {type:"Short",q:"29. What is electrolytic refining?",a:"It is a process of purifying a metal using electrolysis; pure metal deposits at the cathode."},
      {type:"Short",q:"30. Why are gold and platinum often found in the native state?",a:"They are relatively unreactive and can therefore occur naturally in elemental form."},

      {type:"Long",q:"31. Explain how metals react with oxygen, water and dilute acids.",a:"Metals react with oxygen to form metal oxides; for example, 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO. Reactive metals can react with water to form hydroxides or oxides and hydrogen. For example, 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂. Metals above hydrogen in the reactivity series can often displace hydrogen from dilute acids; for example, Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂. Reactivity varies from metal to metal."},
      {type:"Long",q:"32. Explain the formation and properties of ionic compounds using sodium chloride.",a:"Sodium loses one electron to form Na⁺, while chlorine gains one electron to form Cl⁻. Oppositely charged ions attract and form an ionic lattice. Ionic compounds are generally hard, brittle solids with high melting and boiling points. They conduct electricity when molten or dissolved in water because their ions can move, but not usually in the solid state."},
      {type:"Long",q:"33. Explain the extraction of metals according to their reactivity.",a:"Highly reactive metals such as sodium and calcium are generally extracted by electrolytic reduction of molten compounds. Metals of intermediate reactivity, such as iron and zinc, can often be obtained by reducing their oxides with carbon or carbon monoxide. Less reactive metals, such as copper, may be extracted by heating or other suitable processes. The method depends on the metal and its ore."},
      {type:"Long",q:"34. Explain roasting, calcination and electrolytic refining.",a:"Roasting involves heating a sulphide ore strongly in excess air. Calcination involves heating a carbonate ore in limited or no air. Both processes can convert ores into oxides for further extraction. In electrolytic refining, impure metal is the anode, a thin sheet of pure metal is the cathode and a suitable salt solution is the electrolyte. Pure metal deposits at the cathode while insoluble impurities may settle as anode mud."},
      {type:"Long",q:"35. What is corrosion? Explain prevention methods.",a:"Corrosion is the gradual deterioration of a metal through reactions with its surroundings. Iron rusts when exposed to oxygen and moisture. Painting, oiling, greasing, galvanisation and suitable protective coatings reduce contact with corrosive agents. Some metals form protective oxide layers that slow further corrosion."}
