      {type:"Short",q:"30. What is feedback regulation in hormone secretion?",a:"A control mechanism in which changes in a body's condition influence further hormone release, helping maintain stable internal conditions."},

      {type:"Long",q:"31. Explain the structure and working of a neuron.",a:"A neuron consists of a cell body, dendrites and an axon. Dendrites receive signals, and the cell body processes them. An electrical impulse travels along the axon. At a synapse, chemical messengers can transmit the signal to another neuron or an effector cell. This allows information to travel through the nervous system."},
      {type:"Long",q:"32. Explain reflex action with the reflex arc.",a:"A reflex action is a rapid, automatic response to a stimulus. A receptor detects the stimulus and a sensory neuron carries the impulse to the spinal cord. A relay neuron passes the signal to a motor neuron, which carries it to a muscle or gland. The effector responds. The brain may receive information as well, but the rapid response can be coordinated by the spinal cord."},
      {type:"Long",q:"33. Describe the major parts of the human brain and their functions.",a:"The forebrain, including the cerebrum, is involved in thinking, memory, sensory interpretation and voluntary actions. The midbrain helps relay and coordinate certain responses. The hindbrain includes the cerebellum, pons and medulla. The cerebellum coordinates balance and movement; the pons helps regulate breathing; and the medulla controls important involuntary activities such as breathing and heartbeat."},
      {type:"Long",q:"34. Explain hormonal coordination in humans with examples.",a:"Endocrine glands release hormones into the blood. The thyroid releases hormones that regulate metabolism; the pancreas releases insulin to regulate blood glucose; the adrenal glands release adrenaline during emergencies; and the pituitary releases hormones involved in growth and regulation of other glands. Hormone release is often controlled by feedback mechanisms."},
      {type:"Long",q:"35. Explain tropic movements in plants with examples.",a:"Tropic movements are directional growth responses to stimuli. Shoots commonly show positive phototropism by growing towards light. Roots generally show positive geotropism by growing towards gravity, while shoots show negative geotropism. Roots can show positive hydrotropism by growing towards water. Auxin distribution often helps produce unequal growth on opposite sides of a plant organ."}
