      {type:"Short",q:"21. Give two differences between sexual and asexual reproduction.",a:"Asexual reproduction generally involves one parent and no gamete fusion, producing genetically similar offspring. Sexual reproduction involves gametes and usually creates greater genetic variation."},
      {type:"Short",q:"22. Explain binary fission with an example.",a:"In binary fission, one parent cell divides into two daughter cells. Amoeba is a common example."},
      {type:"Short",q:"23. What is budding? Give an example.",a:"A small outgrowth develops on the parent, grows and may separate to form a new organism. Yeast reproduces by budding."},
      {type:"Short",q:"24. Explain vegetative propagation with two examples.",a:"New plants develop from vegetative parts instead of seeds. Potato tubers and the stems of rose plants used for cuttings are examples."},
      {type:"Short",q:"25. Differentiate between pollination and fertilisation.",a:"Pollination is the transfer of pollen from anther to stigma. Fertilisation is the fusion of male and female gametes."},
      {type:"Short",q:"26. What changes occur during puberty?",a:"Reproductive organs mature, hormone production changes and secondary sexual characteristics develop. Growth rate and body proportions may also change."},
      {type:"Short",q:"27. What are the functions of the testes?",a:"Testes produce sperm and secrete testosterone, which contributes to male reproductive development and secondary sexual characteristics."},
      {type:"Short",q:"28. What happens during menstruation?",a:"If pregnancy does not occur, the thickened uterine lining breaks down and is discharged with blood and tissue through the vagina."},
      {type:"Short",q:"29. Why is the placenta important?",a:"It allows exchange of oxygen, nutrients and waste products between the mother and developing fetus and produces hormones that help maintain pregnancy."},
      {type:"Short",q:"30. Why are condoms useful?",a:"They act as a barrier that helps prevent sperm from entering the reproductive tract and also reduce the risk of many sexually transmitted infections."},

      {type:"Long",q:"31. Explain the main methods of asexual reproduction with examples.",a:"Binary fission occurs when one cell divides into two, as in Amoeba. Budding forms a small outgrowth, as in yeast. Fragmentation occurs when an organism splits into parts that grow into individuals, as in Spirogyra under suitable conditions. Some organisms show regeneration, and plants can reproduce by vegetative propagation through stems, roots or leaves."},
      {type:"Long",q:"32. Describe sexual reproduction in flowering plants.",a:"The stamen is the male reproductive part and produces pollen grains. The carpel contains the stigma, style and ovary, which contains ovules. Pollination transfers pollen to the stigma. A pollen tube grows towards an ovule, allowing the male gamete to reach the female gamete. Fertilisation forms a zygote. The ovule develops into a seed and the ovary usually develops into a fruit."},
      {type:"Long",q:"33. Describe the human male and female reproductive systems.",a:"The male system includes testes, sperm ducts, accessory glands, urethra and penis. Testes produce sperm and testosterone. The female system includes ovaries, fallopian tubes, uterus, cervix and vagina. Ovaries produce ova and hormones. Fertilisation usually occurs in a fallopian tube, while embryo implantation and development occur in the uterus."},
      {type:"Long",q:"34. Explain fertilisation, implantation and development in humans.",a:"After ovulation, an ovum enters a fallopian tube. If sperm reaches it, fertilisation may form a zygote. The zygote divides repeatedly while moving towards the uterus. The developing embryo implants in the uterine lining. The placenta supports exchange of nutrients, oxygen and wastes. Development continues in the uterus until birth."},
      {type:"Long",q:"35. Explain the importance of reproductive health and contraception.",a:"Reproductive health includes physical, emotional and social well-being related to reproduction. Reliable information, hygiene, consent, access to healthcare and prevention of sexually transmitted infections are important. Contraceptive methods include barriers, hormonal methods and intrauterine devices; different methods have different benefits and limitations. Condoms can reduce the risk of many sexually transmitted infections as well as pregnancy. Medical advice can help people choose appropriate methods."}
