      {q:"20. Name one practical use of trigonometry.",a:"Estimating the height of a building or tree without measuring it directly.",type:"Very Short"},

      {q:"21. A tower is 10 m high and the observation point is 10 m from its base. Find the angle of elevation.",a:"tan θ = 10/10 = 1. Therefore θ = 45°.",type:"Short"},
      {q:"22. A pole is 12 m high. If tan θ = 3/4 from an observation point, find the horizontal distance.",a:"tan θ = height/distance, so 3/4 = 12/d. Thus d = 16 m.",type:"Short"},
      {q:"23. A tree is observed from 20 m away at an elevation angle of 45°. Find its height above the observer's eye level.",a:"tan 45° = h/20, so h = 20 m.",type:"Short"},
      {q:"24. A kite is 30 m above the observer's eye level. If its angle of elevation is 30°, find the horizontal distance, assuming the string's vertical height is as stated.",a:"tan 30° = 30/d. Thus d = 30√3 m.",type:"Short"},
      {q:"25. From a point 15 m away, the angle of elevation to the top of a pole is 60°. Find its height above eye level.",a:"tan 60° = h/15. Therefore h = 15√3 m.",type:"Short"},
      {q:"26. A building is 20 m high. The angle of elevation from a point on level ground is 45°. Find the distance from the building.",a:"tan 45° = 20/d, so d = 20 m.",type:"Short"},
      {q:"27. Explain why the angle of depression equals the corresponding angle of elevation.",a:"The horizontal lines are parallel. The line of sight acts as a transversal, so the alternate interior angles are equal.",type:"Short"},
      {q:"28. A tower's height above eye level is 8 m and its horizontal distance is 8√3 m. Find the angle of elevation.",a:"tan θ = 8/(8√3) = 1/√3, so θ = 30°.",type:"Short"},
      {q:"29. A 6 m pole casts a horizontal line of sight from a point 6√3 m away. Find the angle of elevation to its top.",a:"tan θ = 6/(6√3) = 1/√3, so θ = 30°.",type:"Short"},
      {q:"30. A tower is observed at 30° elevation from point A. At point B, which is closer to the tower on the same straight level ground, the elevation is larger. Explain.",a:"The height stays fixed while horizontal distance decreases. Since tan θ = height/distance, tan θ increases and therefore the angle increases.",type:"Short"},

      {q:"31. A tower stands on level ground. At a point 20 m from its base, its top has an elevation angle of 60°. Find the tower's height.",a:"Let height be h. tan 60° = h/20. Therefore h = 20√3 m.",type:"Long"},
      {q:"32. From the top of a 24 m building, the angle of depression of a point on level ground is 30°. Find the horizontal distance of the point from the building, assuming the observer's eye is at the stated height.",a:"Let distance be d. tan 30° = 24/d. Therefore d = 24√3 m.",type:"Long"},
      {q:"33. HOTS: Two points on the same straight line with a tower are 20 m apart. The nearer point has elevation 60° and the farther point has elevation 30°. Find the tower's height.",a:"Let the farther distance be x and height be h. From the farther point, h=x/√3. From the nearer point, h=√3(x−20). Equating gives x=30 m, so h=10√3 m.",type:"Long"},
      {q:"34. A person observes the top of a vertical tower at 30°. After walking 20 m towards it, the angle becomes 60°. Find the tower's height.",a:"Let the nearer distance be x and height h. h=x√3 and h=(x+20)/√3. Thus 3x=x+20, so x=10 m and h=10√3 m.",type:"Long"},
      {q:"35. HOTS: From the top of a cliff 30 m high, the angle of depression of a boat is 45°. Find the horizontal distance of the boat from the foot of the cliff.",a:"The angle of depression equals the angle of elevation, 45°. tan 45° = 30/d, so d=30 m.",type:"Long"}
